Q: c++: convert std::chrono of variable unit to seconds A: <p>You may divide any two <code>std::chrono::duration</code>s of any representation or period to efficiently get a scalar count.</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;class Rep, class Period&gt;
constexpr double seconds(std::chrono::duration&lt;Rep,Period&gt; d)
{
    using namespace std::literals;

    // How many seconds?  Divide by one second!
    // The units cancel out and you get a number.
    return d / 1.0s;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The scalar representation will be the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/6503773/what-is-the-purpose-of-stdcommon-type">common type</a> of the two duration representations.</p>
<pre><code>std::chrono::minutes{1} / 1s   -&gt; 60
std::chrono::minutes{1} / 1.0s -&gt; 60.0
</code></pre>
<p>Note that this technique can also give you a &quot;count&quot; of any unusual duration.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>How many 2 minute intervals?</p>
</li>
<li><p>How many 33 nanosecond intervals?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Just divide by that amount.</p>
